            <thead><tr><th>Volume</th><th class="num">JSON-SSE</th><th class="num">Codec</th><th class="num win">Saved</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>1M replies/day</td>     <td class="num">$1,310/mo</td><td class="num">$0.78/mo</td>  <td class="num win"><strong>$1,309/mo</strong></td></tr>
              <tr><td>10M replies/day</td>    <td class="num">$13,100/mo</td><td class="num">$7.80/mo</td> <td class="num win"><strong>$13,092/mo</strong></td></tr>
              <tr><td>100M replies/day</td>   <td class="num">$131,000/mo</td><td class="num">$78/mo</td>  <td class="num win"><strong>~$1.57M/yr</strong></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="cost-cell__foot">
            GCP + Azure egress are in the same ballpark ($0.08&ndash;$0.12/GB). Numbers round; per-reply ratio is fixed by the wire bench at <strong>1,707&times;</strong> &mdash; cost shrinks by the same factor.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- 2. Mobile data + radio energy -->
        <section class="cost-cell">
          <header class="cost-cell__head">
            <span class="cost-cell__tag">mobile + radio</span>
            <h4>Per-response data + battery</h4>
          </header>
          <p class="cost-cell__body">
            Carrier add'l-data rate ~$10/GB. Radio-link energy ~50&nbsp;nJ/bit (conservative cross-tech estimate from published 4G/5G/Wi-Fi measurements). Per 2K-token chat reply:
          </p>
          <table class="cost-table">
            <thead><tr><th></th><th class="num">JSON-SSE</th><th class="num">Codec</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Data cost</td>      <td class="num">$0.0049</td><td class="num">$0.000003</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Radio energy</td>   <td class="num">194 mJ</td>  <td class="num">0.12 mJ</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Bits over the air</td><td class="num">3.88 Mb</td><td class="num">2.3 Kb</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="cost-cell__foot">
            Per-response cost is tiny on a phone; fleet-scale meaningful. 1B chat replies/day across a mobile fleet: ~194&nbsp;MJ vs ~0.12&nbsp;MJ on radio links &mdash; <strong>~54&nbsp;kWh/day</strong> saved at the airlink alone (about <strong>~1.8 average US households' daily electricity</strong> &mdash; EIA average is ~30&nbsp;kWh/household/day), plus the per-user battery + data-cap relief. The full non-GPU energy delta (radio + network + client CPU) is bigger &mdash; see the power+latency card below.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- 3. Client CPU -->
        <section class="cost-cell">
          <header class="cost-cell__head">
            <span class="cost-cell__tag">client CPU</span>
            <h4>Tokenize / detokenize removed from the hot path</h4>
          </header>
          <p class="cost-cell__body">
            Two measured points from the bench section above, both worth real CPU on the consumer side:
          </p>
          <ul class="cost-bullets">
            <li><strong>ToolWatcher</strong> &mdash; <code>2.08 ns/token</code> (single 32-bit compare) vs <code>55.42 ns/token</code> for detokenize+regex match. <strong>26.7&times;</strong> less CPU on every tool-detection pass.</li>
            <li><strong>mcp-leaf</strong> &mdash; <code>0.004 ms</code> meta-read vs <code>0.052 ms</code> BPE tokenize per tool result. <strong>12.4&times;</strong> less CPU per tool call where the result includes <code>_meta['ai.codec/leaf-tokenization']</code>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="cost-cell__foot">
            Per call it's microseconds. At fleet scale (100M consumers &times; 100 tool-bearing turns/day) the agent-mesh saves on the order of <strong>~1,000 CPU-hours/day</strong> across the consumer fleet. On a single laptop running an agent loop locally: less fan noise, longer battery.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- 4. Server-side inference cost (honest framing) -->
        <section class="cost-cell">
          <header class="cost-cell__head">
            <span class="cost-cell__tag cost-cell__tag--warn">server CPU floor</span>
            <h4>What Codec does NOT change: GPU compute</h4>
          </header>
          <p class="cost-cell__body">
            The model still runs at the same TPS. Codec doesn't accelerate token generation, doesn't change weights, doesn't change KV-cache footprint. <strong>If you're GPU-bound, Codec is invisible to your $/token.</strong>
          </p>
          <p class="cost-cell__body">
            What it DOES reduce on the server: response-side <em>serialize</em> + UTF-8 encode + per-token JSON envelope. That's typically 1&ndash;5% of server CPU on a busy node and dominates only at high concurrency. The practical effect is the <em>concurrent-request ceiling per GPU</em> moves up &mdash; lab measurements on sglang show ~5&ndash;10% more sustained concurrent streams before TTFB degrades, because the response-builder thread isn't UTF-8-encoding every token. That translates to fewer GPUs needed to hit the same QPS target, not faster individual responses.
          </p>
          <p class="cost-cell__foot cost-cell__foot--caveat">
            Caveat: this is observation from the bench rig, not a controlled GPU-utilisation A/B against vanilla. Treat as directional &mdash; the safe claim is "Codec doesn't slow down inference and removes a known serialize bottleneck at concurrency." We do NOT claim a $/token reduction on the model itself.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>

      <footer class="bench-card__foot">
        Source numbers: cross-stack matrix (wire bytes), <code>leaf-live.ts</code> (consumer CPU), <code>bench_watcher.c</code> (ToolWatcher ns/token). Egress rates: <a href="https://aws.amazon.com/s3/pricing/" rel="noopener">AWS S3 standard tier</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/storage/pricing#network-egress" rel="noopener">GCP equivalent</a>. Radio-link energy: ~50&nbsp;nJ/bit conservative cross-tech estimate (published 4G/5G/Wi-Fi measurements vary 30&ndash;100 nJ/bit). All cost translations are back-of-envelope from public rates &times; measured bytes &mdash; production deployments should re-run with their own egress contract + traffic mix.
      </footer>
    </article>

