      <h4 class="bench-subhead bench-subhead--secondary">Energy budget per response</h4>
      <p class="bench-subhead-note">Where the joules actually go. Conservative estimates from published per-bit + per-Mtok values multiplied through the v0.4.1 wire numbers.</p>

      <div class="power-grid">
        <table class="cost-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Component</th>
              <th class="num">JSON-SSE</th>
              <th class="num">Codec</th>
              <th class="num">Ratio</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>GPU compute (Qwen-2.5 0.5B, 2K tokens)</td>    <td class="num">~5&ndash;15&nbsp;J</td>  <td class="num">~5&ndash;15&nbsp;J</td>      <td class="num">unchanged</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Server CPU (response serialize + UTF-8 encode)</td><td class="num">~10&nbsp;mJ</td>    <td class="num">~0.5&nbsp;mJ</td>          <td class="num win">~20&times;</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Network end-to-end (~150 nJ/bit)</td>          <td class="num">~580&nbsp;mJ</td>   <td class="num">~0.35&nbsp;mJ</td>          <td class="num win">~1,660&times;</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Client radio (mobile, ~50&nbsp;nJ/bit)</td>    <td class="num">~194&nbsp;mJ</td>   <td class="num">~0.12&nbsp;mJ</td>          <td class="num win">~1,617&times;</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Client CPU (BPE tokenize + JSON parse)</td>    <td class="num">~50&nbsp;mJ</td>    <td class="num">~4&nbsp;mJ</td>             <td class="num win">~12&times;</td></tr>
            <tr class="agent-loop-delta"><td><strong>Per-response total (mobile path)</strong></td>
                                                                   <td class="num"><strong>~6&nbsp;J + 824&nbsp;mJ</strong></td>
                                                                   <td class="num"><strong>~6&nbsp;J + 5&nbsp;mJ</strong></td>
                                                                   <td class="num">GPU dominant; everything else <strong>~165&times;</strong> less</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="cost-cell__body" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
        <strong>Fleet scale &mdash; what these numbers mean at AI-provider volume:</strong>
        for 100M chat replies/day on mobile, the non-GPU energy delta is
        ~82 MJ/day = <strong>~23&nbsp;kWh/day</strong> saved (radio +
        network + client CPU) &mdash; <strong>~0.8 US households' daily electricity</strong>
        (EIA average ~30&nbsp;kWh/household/day). At
        <strong>~1B replies/day &mdash; the rough order of magnitude
        public estimates place a major AI provider's 2026 fleet at
        (Anthropic Claude across web + API + Code, OpenAI ChatGPT, etc.;
        none publish exact numbers)</strong> &mdash; the delta is
        <strong>~230&nbsp;kWh/day</strong> = <strong>~7-8 US households powered for a day</strong>,
        or an electric car's worth of charging, every day. GPU compute
        keeps drawing the same wattage either way &mdash; if you're
        trying to reduce inference power, that's a model + accelerator
        question, not a wire-protocol question.
      </p>

      <footer class="bench-card__foot">
        TTFB numbers from MATRIX.md §4 (Python httpx <code>aiter_raw</code> first-body-byte median). Agent-loop wall-clock from <code>results/2026-05-15T20-00-00Z/agent-loop/</code> &mdash; wire numbers measured, not extrapolated; speedups are real wall-clock from POST to final answer. Energy estimates: GPU from published per-token figures for small models on Ampere; network end-to-end ~150&nbsp;nJ/bit (datacenter switching + transit + last-mile, conservative); radio link ~50&nbsp;nJ/bit (4G/5G/Wi-Fi midpoint); server CPU response-serialize from libcodec microbench scaled to msgpack-encode equivalent; client CPU from tokenize/detok microbench. Mileage varies per deployment.
      </footer>
